  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Roxane's bolete (Aureoboletus roxanae) is a striking fungus with a distinctive golden-yellow color that becomes more orange or reddish with age and bruising. This medium-sized bolete is found in New England forests and is notable for its bright coloration and blue-green staining reactions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "Golden-yellow to orange-yellow cap that often develops reddish or rusty tones, especially at the margins or where bruised",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface Reaction",
      "description": "Yellow pores on the underside that stain blue or blue-green when bruised or cut",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Characteristics",
      "description": "Yellow stem with red or orange coloring, especially toward the base, creating a two-toned appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Roxane's bolete fruiting bodies appear on forest floors in deciduous and mixed woodlands throughout New England, typically during summer and fall months.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'roxanae' honors Roxane, and this bolete's dramatic color changes and blue-staining reactions make it a favorite among mushroom foragers and photographers, though it is not considered edible.",
